===1957 to present===
In 1956, the United States, Canada, and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The 1956 (dated 1957) issue was the first Connecticut license plate that complied with these standards.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ctplates.info/ct_pass50-59.html |title=Illustrated History of Connecticut License Plates – Passenger 1950–1959 |last=Wasielewski |first=Joe |access-date=June 23, 2017}}</ref>

Plates continued to be validated with tabs each year through 1960. Monthly staggered registration commenced in 1961; tabs were used for expirations from May 1962 through February 1963, and stickers thereafter.{{citation needed|date = June 2017}}

The "Constitution State" slogan was first used on the 1974 blue-on-white base. This base did not hold up well and was discontinued in 1976; remaining plates were recalled in 1990 and replaced with white-on-blue plates bearing the same serials.{{citation needed|date = June 2017}}

From 1980 through 1987, only rear plates were issued as a cost-saving measure. When front plates became mandatory again in 1987, the design of the 1976 white-on-blue base was changed in order to distinguish between plates issued in pairs and rear-only plates, with a state shape added in the top left corner and the "Constitution State" slogan moved from the top to the bottom.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ctplates.info/ct_pass80-89.html |title=Illustrated History of Connecticut License Plates – Passenger 1980–1989 |last=Wasielewski |first=Joe |access-date=June 23, 2017}}</ref>

The current sky blue base was introduced in January 2000. Between September 2000 and August 2002, all remaining 1957–74 and 1976–99 plates were replaced with plates on this base bearing the same serials. Hence, serials issued in 1957 can be seen on the current base.

Plate stickers continued to be issued through August 2006 (the last being used for August 2008 expirations). From September 2006 through July 2010, windshield stickers were used instead (the last being used for July 2012 expirations); these were discontinued after the Connecticut Department of Motor Vehicles began allowing police agencies to use license plate scanners to verify registrations.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Kauffman |first1=Matthew |title=Police keeping data from license plate scans; ACLU files privacy protest |url=https://www.courant.com/2012/02/21/police-keeping-data-from-license-plate-scans-aclu-files-privacy-protest/ |work=Hartford Courant |date=February 21, 2012 |access-date=September 21, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Tepfer |first1=Daniel |title=Police tout use of license plate scanners |url=http://www.ctpost.com/local/article/Police-tout-use-of-license-plate-scanners-3453946.php |work=Connecticut Post |date=April 2, 2012 |access-date=September 21, 2015}}</ref>

The older 123·ABC format was in use from 1980 to 2013 - on both the 1976 and 2000 bases. After its exhaustion, the 1ABCD2 and 1AB·CD2 formats were used, until a computer upgrade was completed in 2015. Since then, Connecticut has used the seven-character AB·12345 format, beginning from AA·00001. Plates progressed to the current "B" series in 2020.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Connecticut 2015 Series Series History |url=https://www.licenseplates.cc/history.php?id=3188 |access-date=2026-01-02 |website=www.licenseplates.cc}}</ref>

